# Introduction to Linux
Linux is a free, open-source operating system based on the Unix architecture. It was first created in 1991 by Linus Torvalds and has since grown into one of the most widely used operating systems in the world.
🔑 Key Features
Open Source: Anyone can view, modify, and distribute the source code
Multiuser & Multitasking: Multiple users and processes can run at the same time
Security: Strong permissions and user roles
Stability: Rare crashes; widely used in servers
Portability: Runs on everything from phones to supercomputers
🌍Where Linux is Used
Servers (most of the internet runs on Linux)
Cloud computing platforms
Android devices (based on Linux kernel)
Embedded systems (smart TVs, routers)
Supercomputers
🚀 Why Learn Linux?
Essential for careers in IT, cybersecurity, and software development
Gives full control over your system
Widely used in cloud platforms like AWS and Azure
Powerful for automation and scripting
🖥️ Basic Components
Kernel – the core of Linux that manages hardware and processes
Shell – interface to interact with the system (e.g., terminal)
File System – hierarchical structure (/, /home, /etc, etc.)
Utilities – tools and commands (like ls, cp, mv)

# History of Linux
-In the beginning, there was an operating system called Unix created in 1969 at Bell Labs.
-Unix became popular because it was fast, stable, and could support many users.
-In 1983, Richard Stallman started the GNU Project to make free software for everyone.
-In 1991, a student named Linus Torvalds created the Linux kernel as a small personal project.
-He shared Linux on the internet for free, and many programmers started improving it.
- Over time, Linux became very popular and different versions called distributions were created, such as:
Ubuntu
Debian
Fedora
- Today, Linux is used in:
Servers
Computers
Smartphones through Android
Supercomputers
Cloud systems

