# Linux File System Structure
The Linux file system is organized in a tree-like structure.
Everything starts from the root directory represented by /.

🌳 Basic Structure
/
├── bin
├── boot
├── dev
├── etc
├── home
├── lib
├── media
├── mnt
├── opt
├── proc
├── root
├── sbin
├── tmp
├── usr
└── var

📁 Important Directories
🔹 / (Root Directory)
Main directory of Linux
All files and folders start from here
Example:
/
🔹 /home
Stores personal files of users
Each user gets a separate folder
Example:
/home/user1
🔹 /bin
Contains basic commands and executable files
Examples:
ls
cp
mv
🔹 /etc
Stores system configuration files
Examples:
network settings
user settings
🔹 /boot
Contains boot loader files
Helps Linux start during booting
🔹 /dev
Contains device files
Represents hardware devices
Examples:
USB
hard disk
keyboard
🔹 /tmp
Stores temporary files
Files may be deleted automatically after restart
🔹 /usr
Contains software and user applications
Large directory in Linux
🔹 /var
Stores variable data like:
logs
mail
cache
🔹 /root
Home directory of the root (administrator) user
📌 Features of Linux File System
Hierarchical structure
Everything is treated as a file
Supports permissions and security
Multiuser support
Organized data storage

