# File Permissions & Ownership
File Permissions and Ownership are very important concepts in Linux.
They control:

Who can access a file
Who can modify a file
Who can execute a file
Who owns the file

These features provide security and access control in Linux systems.

1. What are File Permissions?
Every file and directory in Linux has permissions attached to it.

Permissions decide what actions users can perform on a file or directory.
There are 3 types of permissions:

Permission	   Symbol             	Meaning
Read          	 r	           View file content
Write	w	Modify   or            edit file
Execute	        x	      Run the file as a program/script

> File permissions are used for:
Server security
Script execution
Application deployment
User management
Docker and Kubernetes
SSH key protection

# Users and Groups Management
User and group management is the process of controlling who can access a system and what they are allowed to do.
Users:-
A user is an individual account that can log into a system.

Typical user operations:
Create users
Modify user details
Disable or delete users

Example:
sudo useradd john
sudo passwd john

Groups:-
A group is a collection of users with shared permissions.
Benefits:
Easier permission management
Role-based access control
Shared access to files/resources
Example:
sudo groupadd developers
sudo usermod -aG developers john

