> APT (Advanced Package Tool)
Definition-
APT (Advanced Package Tool) is a package manager used in Debian-based distributions such as Ubuntu and Debian to manage .deb packages.

Features
Fast package installation
Automatic dependency management
Repository support
Easy software updates
Important Commands

Update Package List:
sudo apt update

Install Package:
sudo apt install nginx

Remove Package:
sudo apt remove nginx

> YUM (Yellowdog Updater Modified)
Definition-
YUM Red Hat-based Linux systems (CentOS 7, RHEL 7) ka package manager hai jo .rpm packages ko manage karta hai.

Features
Dependency resolution
Repository management
Package updates
Easy software installation
Important Commands

Install Package:
sudo yum install httpd

Update Packages:
sudo yum update

Remove Package:
sudo yum remove httpd

> DNF (Dandified YUM)
Definition-
DNF YUM ka improved version hai jo Fedora, RHEL 8+, CentOS 8+, Rocky Linux aur AlmaLinux me use hota hai.

Features
Faster performance
Better dependency management
Less memory usage
Improved security
Important Commands

Install Package:
sudo dnf install nginx

Update Packages:
sudo dnf update

Remove Package:
sudo dnf remove nginx

Example
Ubuntu (APT)-
Install Nginx:
sudo apt update          // terminal
sudo apt install nginx   // terminal

CentOS 7 (YUM)-
Install Apache:
sudo yum install httpd

RHEL 8 (DNF)-
Install Nginx:
sudo dnf install nginx   // for terminal


# Process Management
Process Management is the activity of creating, scheduling, monitoring, controlling, and terminating processes in an operating system. It helps the CPU execute multiple programs efficiently.

A process is a program that is currently running in memory.

Key Functions of Process Management
1. Process Creation
The operating system creates a new process when a program is executed.
Example: Opening Google Chrome creates a new process.

2. Process Scheduling
The CPU decides which process should run next.
Example: While listening to music and browsing the web, the OS allocates CPU time to both applications.

3. Process Synchronization
Coordinates multiple processes to avoid conflicts when accessing shared resources.
Example: Multiple users accessing the same database.

4. Process Communication
Processes exchange information with each other.
Example: A browser communicating with a web server.

5. Process Termination
The OS removes a process after it completes execution or is stopped.
Example: Closing Microsoft Word terminates its process.

Process States
New – Process is being created.
Ready – Waiting for CPU allocation.
Running – Currently executing.
Waiting/Blocked – Waiting for I/O operation.
Terminated – Execution completed.

